00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:14.655
OK, let's discuss compilation.

00:02:14.655 --> 00:02:16.030
The first thing
we should discuss

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:18.340
are the differences between
Java standard just-in-time

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:20.560
compilation, and the
ahead-of-time compilation

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:22.450
that we'll be ending with today.

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:25.880
When developers run Java
C, normally a compile time,

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:29.770
we turn our .Java source
code into .class byte code.

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:31.210
These class files
can only really

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:33.710
be understood by the Java
virtual machine itself,

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:36.610
so before our code can actually
be executed on our server

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:40.360
at runtime, the JVM must
interpret or further compile it

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.340
into native code
just in time for it

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:44.530
to be executed at runtime.

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:47.890
This approach has one big
advantage and one big drawback.

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:50.350
On the one hand, we get to
write our code once and run it

00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:52.283
everywhere, the
popular Java tag line,

00:02:52.283 --> 00:02:53.950
and that gives us
portability because we

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:55.325
know that the JVM
will figure out

00:02:55.325 --> 00:02:57.010
how to execute it
on whichever system

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:58.600
we end up deploying it to.

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:00.700
On the other hand, the
interpretation step

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:03.580
is quite expensive when compared
to just running native code

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:05.240
directly.

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:07.310
As a result, we often end
up trading performance

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:09.020
for portability,
even in situations

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:11.780
where that portability
doesn't buy us all that much.

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:14.360
Ahead-of-time compilation
takes the opposite approach

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:17.660
by compiling all reachable
code into a native executable

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:19.640
at compile time so
that we can simply run

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:21.260
our code on the target machine.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:23.750
The result here is a trade
off in the opposite direction,

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:25.790
giving up portability in
exchange for a smaller

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:28.610
application that executes
our code directly at runtime

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:30.620
without the need for
further compilation,

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:34.310
meaning that your app can start
up faster and uses less memory.

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:37.020
Of course, each approach can
shine in their own use cases.

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:38.870
The standard JVM is
very well optimized

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:41.570
for long-running processes,
while AOT compilation

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:43.310
is great for
short-lived workloads

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:46.730
where a fast startup
time is important.

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:49.190
As a fun fact, AOT
compilation was introduced

00:03:49.190 --> 00:03:51.140
as an experimental
feature in JDK 9,

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:54.020
though its implementation
was expensive to maintain,

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:56.870
and it never quite caught
on, so it was quietly

00:03:56.870 --> 00:03:59.300
removed in Java 17 in
favor of developers

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:01.400
just using GraalVM,
which is what

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:03.420
Spring Native makes use of.

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:06.210
Speaking of GraalVM,
what exactly is it?

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:10.290
GraalVM is a highly-optimized,
open source JDK distribution

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:12.600
that boasts extremely
fast startup times,

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:15.480
AOT native compilation,
and polyglot capabilities

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:18.240
that allow developers to
mix multiple languages

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.430
into a single application.

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:22.200
Graal is an active
development with lots

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:25.140
of notable milestones, but the
one I'll quickly highlight here

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:27.690
is Java 17 support,
which is pretty recent,

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:31.500
having only happened
on January 18, 2022.

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:33.720
So to be a little bit more
specific with our earlier

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:35.760
Spring Native
definition, Spring Native

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:38.550
enables the use of GraalVM's
native image compiler

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:41.670
to turn Spring applications
into native executables.

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:43.890
This process involves
performing a static analysis

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:45.390
of your application
at compile time

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:47.430
to find all methods
and configuration

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:50.537
in your application that are
reachable from the entry point.

00:04:50.537 --> 00:04:52.620
This essentially creates
a closed-world conception

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:54.150
of your application,
where all code

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:56.040
is assumed to be
known at compile time

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:59.130
and no new code is allowed
to be loaded at runtime.

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:01.590
It's important to note that
native image generation is

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:04.170
a memory intensive process that
takes longer than compiling

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:06.780
a regular application
and imposes limitations

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:08.790
on certain aspects of Java.

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:11.010
In some cases, no
actual code changes

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:13.560
are required for an application
to work with Spring Native.

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:16.320
However, there are some things
like dynamic class loading

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:19.350
or dynamic proxies that need
to be enumerated or configured

00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:21.893
ahead of time in order to work.

00:05:21.893 --> 00:05:23.310
In those situations,
Spring Native

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:24.690
provides things
like native hints

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:27.160
to simplify the process,
which are well documented.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:27.660
All right.

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:29.035
With all that
background covered,

00:05:29.035 --> 00:05:31.200
we can finally write some code.

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:33.180
Before we start
implementing Spring Native,

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:35.390
we'll need to create and
deploy an app to establish

00:05:35.390 --> 00:05:37.140
a performance baseline
that we can compare

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:39.690
to the native version later.

00:05:39.690 --> 00:05:41.940
Today, we'll be going through
this on the Cloud Shell,

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:43.733
on a new Google Cloud
project to minimize

00:05:43.733 --> 00:05:45.900
the configuration required,
but you can follow along

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:47.067
on your own machine as well.

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:54.940
We'll start by getting our
app from start.spring.io.

00:05:54.940 --> 00:05:57.603
The starter app uses
Spring Boot 2.6.4,

00:05:57.603 --> 00:05:59.020
which is the latest
version that's

00:05:59.020 --> 00:06:02.480
supported by the Spring Native
project at time of writing.

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:05.750
Note that since the
release of GraalVM 21.0.3,

00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:08.660
you could use Java 17
for the sample as well.

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:10.460
We will be sticking
with Java 11 today

00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:13.682
to minimize the config
involved though.

00:06:13.682 --> 00:06:15.390
Once we have our zip
on the command line,

00:06:15.390 --> 00:06:17.280
we can create a
sub-directory for our project

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:18.720
and unzip the folder in there.

00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:23.960
Once we have the project
opened in our IDE of choice,

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:25.473
I'll be using
Cloud editor, we'll

00:06:25.473 --> 00:06:27.140
quickly add a sign
of life and something

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:31.380
to showcase the Spring Native
