00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:37.530
suggestions when I
first opened my source

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:38.790
directory as a Workspace.

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:48.500
All we're doing here is
adding two fields, start time

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:50.630
and ready time, to help
us understand the startup

00:06:50.630 --> 00:06:53.660
time once we run our
application, instructing

00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:55.910
the main function to
save the start time,

00:06:55.910 --> 00:06:58.610
then using the application
ready event to log when Spring

00:06:58.610 --> 00:07:00.852
says our application is ready.

00:07:00.852 --> 00:07:02.310
Once we have those
two times, we're

00:07:02.310 --> 00:07:04.200
ready to take the
duration between them

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:08.240
and display the startup time
when a user visits our app.

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:10.670
At this point, our baseline
app is ready to go.

00:07:10.670 --> 00:07:12.740
So feel free to build an
image and run it locally

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:14.540
in order to get an idea
of the startup time

00:07:14.540 --> 00:07:17.890
before we convert it to
a native application.

00:07:17.890 --> 00:07:20.440
To build our image, we can run
the Spring Boot Build-Image

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:21.160
Maven Goal.

00:07:25.150 --> 00:07:26.650
Once that's built,
you can also use

00:07:26.650 --> 00:07:29.260
something like Dive
or Docker images demo

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:31.480
to get an idea of the size
of the baseline image.

00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:36.560
And if we want to run our
locally-built regular Docker

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:38.180
image, we can do
so at Docker Run.

00:07:43.712 --> 00:07:45.420
Now that we have our
app, we'll deploy it

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:47.128
and take note of the
startup times, which

00:07:47.128 --> 00:07:49.590
will compare to our native
app startup times later on.

00:07:51.872 --> 00:07:54.080
Depending on the type of
application you're building,

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:56.780
there are several options
for hosting your stuff.

00:07:56.780 --> 00:07:59.390
However, since our example is
a very simple, straightforward

00:07:59.390 --> 00:08:01.730
web application, we can
keep things really simple

00:08:01.730 --> 00:08:02.990
and just rely on Cloud Run.

00:08:02.990 --> 00:08:04.907
If you're following along
on your own machine,

00:08:04.907 --> 00:08:09.640
make sure to have the gcloud
CLI tool installed an updated.

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:12.010
Since this project is still
in the experimental phase,

00:08:12.010 --> 00:08:13.552
we'll have to
configure our app to be

00:08:13.552 --> 00:08:15.850
able to find experimental
artifacts, which are not

00:08:15.850 --> 00:08:19.580
available in Maven
Central Repository.

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:22.272
This will involve making changes
to our palm, which you can do

00:08:22.272 --> 00:08:23.480
in the editor of your choice.

00:08:26.590 --> 00:08:28.840
Next, we'll need to bring
in the dependencies required

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:31.150
to run a Spring application
as a native image.

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:32.710
Note that this step
is not necessary

00:08:32.710 --> 00:08:34.965
if you're using Gradle.

00:08:34.965 --> 00:08:36.590
Now, we'll add the
ahead of time plugin

00:08:36.590 --> 00:08:38.870
to improve native image
compatibility and artifact

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:39.470
footprint.

00:08:42.822 --> 00:08:44.780
And now, we'll update
Spring Boot Mavin plug-in

00:08:44.780 --> 00:08:48.110
to enable native image support
and use the Marketo builder

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:50.990
to build our native image.

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:53.960
Note that the tiny builder image
is just one of several options.

00:08:53.960 --> 00:08:55.430
It's a good choice
for our use case

00:08:55.430 --> 00:08:58.250
because it has very few extra
libraries and utilities,

00:08:58.250 --> 00:09:00.327
which helps us to minimize
our attack surface.

00:09:00.327 --> 00:09:01.910
But if, for example,
you were building

00:09:01.910 --> 00:09:04.735
an app that needed access
to some common C libraries

00:09:04.735 --> 00:09:06.860
or you weren't yet sure of
your app's requirements,

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:09.823
then the full builder
might be a better fit.

00:09:09.823 --> 00:09:11.240
Now, that our apps
are configured,

00:09:11.240 --> 00:09:12.740
we should be able
to build our image

00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:14.622
and run our native compiled app.

00:09:14.622 --> 00:09:16.580
Before running the build,
here are a few things

00:09:16.580 --> 00:09:17.723
to keep in mind.

00:09:17.723 --> 00:09:19.640
This will take more time
than a regular build.

00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:21.170
Think a few minutes.

00:09:21.170 --> 00:09:23.030
This build process can
take a lot of memory.

00:09:23.030 --> 00:09:25.280
Think a few gigabytes.

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:28.653
This build process requires the
Docker daemon to be reachable.

00:09:28.653 --> 00:09:31.070
And while in this example,
we're going through the process

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:33.440
to manually build,
you can also configure

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:35.270
you build phases to
automatically trigger

00:09:35.270 --> 00:09:37.450
a native build profile.

00:09:37.450 --> 00:09:39.140
With that in mind,
just like before,

00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:41.860
we can build our app with the
Spring Boot Build-Image Goal.

00:09:46.390 --> 00:09:48.753
And once that's built, we
can run it the same way.

00:09:48.753 --> 00:09:50.420
At this point, we're
in a great position

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:53.515
to see both sides of the
native application equation.

00:09:53.515 --> 00:09:55.640
We've given up a bit of
time and extra memory usage

00:09:55.640 --> 00:09:57.500
at compile time,
but in exchange, we

00:09:57.500 --> 00:09:59.750
get an application that can
start up far more quickly

00:09:59.750 --> 00:10:01.610
and consume significantly
less memory,

00:10:01.610 --> 00:10:05.040
depending on the workload.

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:07.297
If we run something like
Dive or Docker Images Demo

00:10:07.297 --> 00:10:09.630
to compare the size of the
native image to the original,

00:10:09.630 --> 00:10:14.520
we can see a dramatic
reduction in the image's size.

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:16.720
We should also note that
in more complex use cases,

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:18.510
there are additional
modifications needed

00:10:18.510 --> 00:10:20.460
to inform the AOT
compiler of what

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:22.470
your app will do at runtime.

00:10:22.470 --> 00:10:24.520
For that reason, certain
predictable workloads,

00:10:24.520 --> 00:10:27.450
like batch jobs may be very well
suited to this, while others

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:29.620
could be a bigger lift.

00:10:29.620 --> 00:10:31.480
In order to deploy
our app to cloud run,

00:10:31.480 --> 00:10:33.070
we'll need to get
our native image

00:10:33.070 --> 00:10:38.595
into an image store or package
manager like artifact registry.

00:10:38.595 --> 00:10:43.615
We can get started on this
by creating a repository,

00:10:43.615 --> 00:10:45.240
then we'll need to
make sure that we're

00:10:45.240 --> 00:10:48.150
authenticated to push
images to our new registry.

00:10:48.150 --> 00:10:52.578
The gcloud CLI simplifies
this process quite a bit.

00:10:52.578 --> 00:10:53.620
Now, we'll tag our image.

00:10:57.480 --> 00:10:59.255
Finally, we can use
Docker Push to send it

00:10:59.255 --> 00:11:00.130
to artifact registry.

00:11:02.682 --> 00:11:04.640
We are now ready to take
the native application

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:06.560
image that we've stored
in artifact registry

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:09.055
and deploy it to Cloud Run.

00:11:09.055 --> 00:11:11.430
Since we've built and deployed
our app as a native image,

00:11:11.430 --> 00:11:13.222
we can rest assured
that our application is

00:11:13.222 --> 00:11:15.600
making excellent use of
our infrastructure costs

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:16.125
as it runs.

00:11:18.752 --> 00:11:20.460
And now that our native
service is ready,

00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:25.120
we can compare it's startup
time to the original.

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:26.890
Congratulations on
building and deploying

00:11:26.890 --> 00:11:29.572
a Spring Native application
on Google Cloud.

00:11:29.572 --> 00:11:31.030
Hopefully, this
tutorial encourages

00:11:31.030 --> 00:11:33.363
you to get more familiar with
the Spring Native project,

00:11:33.363 --> 00:11:36.403
and keep it in mind should it
meet your needs in the future.

00:11:36.403 --> 00:11:39.070
Whether you created a new Google
Cloud project for this workshop

00:11:39.070 --> 00:11:41.410
or are reusing an
existing one, take care

00:11:41.410 --> 00:11:43.990
to avoid unnecessary charges
from the resources we made use

00:11:43.990 --> 00:11:45.540
of.

